---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/benchmarks.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const { suite, tests, targets } = data;
const [x86, arm] = suite.machines;
const kernels = [...suite.kernels].sort((a, b) => b.x86 - a.x86);
const atGoal = kernels.filter((kernel) => kernel.x86 >= targets.release && kernel.arm >= targets.release).length;
// Bars stop at 150%. A kernel past that shows its number, and its bar is full.
const scale = 150;
const at = (percent: number) => `${(Math.min(percent, scale) / scale) * 100}%`;
// Build times are shown in milliseconds, as CI recorded them.
const ms = (value: number) => value.toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: 1 });
---

<Base title="Benchmarks" description="Fennec's real benchmark numbers: native release builds against hand-written twins, measured in CI.">
  <section class="wrap head">
    <span class="pill">In progress</span>
    <h1>Benchmarks</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every kernel is built as a native program and raced against a hand-written twin in a fast, C-class systems language. These are real numbers from CI, from commit <code>{suite.commit}</code> on {suite.date}.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Speed against the twin</h2>
    <p class="note">
      100% means as fast as the twin. Across the suite, release builds run at {x86.time}% of the twins' speed on {x86.label}, and {arm.time}% on {arm.label}. That's kernel time, the work itself, as the geometric mean of each kernel's best of ten runs. Startup is still being cut, so whole runs are slower than this. The goal is {targets.release}% on every kernel, and {atGoal} of {kernels.length} are there on both machines. The twins are built fully optimized, with overflow checks, as Fennec always has.
    </p>
    <div class="table">
      <div class="tr th">
        <span>Kernel</span><span>x86-64 Linux</span><span>Arm macOS</span>
      </div>
      {
        kernels.map((kernel) => (
          <div class="tr">
            <span class="name">{kernel.name}</span>
            {[kernel.x86, kernel.arm].map((percent) => (
              <span class="bar-cell">
                <span class="track">
                  <span class="fill" style={`width: ${at(percent)}`} />
                  <span class="mark" style={`left: ${at(100)}`} title="As fast as the twin" />
                  <span class="mark goal" style={`left: ${at(targets.release)}`} title={`The goal: ${targets.release}%`} />
                </span>
                <strong>{percent.toFixed(1)}%</strong>
              </span>
            ))}
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Each kernel runs ten times beside its twin, taking turns, and the best run of each counts. On shared CI machines a single kernel can move by several percent between runs. {x86.slowest === arm.slowest ? <>The slowest kernel on both machines is <code>{x86.slowest}</code>.</> : <>The slowest kernels are <code>{x86.slowest}</code> on x86-64 and <code>{arm.slowest}</code> on Arm.</>} Development builds, which skip whole-program optimization, reach {x86.dev}% and {arm.dev}%.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Build times</h2>
    <p class="note">Each kernel is built from an empty cache, so nothing helps it, in the same CI run.</p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><strong>{ms(suite.dev_build_ms[0])}–{ms(suite.dev_build_ms[1])} ms</strong><span>for a development build of one kernel</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><strong>{ms(suite.release_build_ms[0])}–{ms(suite.release_build_ms[1])} ms</strong><span>for a release build, which optimizes the whole program as one</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><strong>&lt;{tests.unchanged_s} s</strong><span>to rerun tests when nothing they depend on changed, on one small test file, measured on {tests.machine} on {tests.date}</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>How it's measured</h2>
    <p>
      Each kernel stands for one way a language has to be fast: calls, float math, hash tables, strings, allocation, generics, closures and object-heavy code. Each twin does the same work and must print the same answer. Inputs are read so neither optimizer can see through them, and every result depends on all the work. A slow kernel is fixed in the compiler, never by changing the benchmark. The kernels and the method are in <a href="https://github.com/fennec-language/fennec/tree/main/bench">the repository</a>, and you can{' '}
      <a href={`${base}speed/`}>read how Fennec gets fast</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }

  .head {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  .pill {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--accent);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.75rem;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 7vw, 3.6rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: 0.75rem 0;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 640px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .note {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .table {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }

  .tr {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 2fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.7rem 1.1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .tr:first-child {
    border-top: 0;
  }

  .th {
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .name {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .bar-cell {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .bar-cell strong {
    width: 3.5rem;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .mark {
    position: absolute;
    top: -3px;
    bottom: -3px;
    width: 2px;
    background: var(--text, currentColor);
  }

  .mark.goal {
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  .track {
    position: relative;
    flex: 1;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--surface-strong);
  }

  .fill {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 auto 0 0;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--accent-bright);
  }

  .stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .stat {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.4rem;
  }

  .stat strong {
    display: block;
    font-size: 2rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .stat span {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .tr {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 0.35rem 1rem;
    }

    .th {
      display: none;
    }

    .name,
    .bar-cell {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>
